Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=(), event=Decided(decision='instantsutc', at=datetime(2026, 10, 6, 0, 0, 0), actor='assistant', title="An instant is a UTC point in time; a day is the owner's, in a named zone; an older bundle is refused with 426", body='*Wall clocks.* `at` is the writer\'s clock, and a writer is a PHONE: nothing clamps what it claims (SPEC §1). An instant MEANS A POINT IN TIME: naive and UTC by definition, stamped so by every writer (the box\'s one `Clock`, the tab\'s `time.moment`) and read so by every reader (the tab\'s one `time.ms`; a guard refuses `chrono::Local`). A DAY is the owner\'s, in `[persona] zone`, a NAMED zone (`zone::Zone`, the deployment\'s, handed to the tab by `/api/me`), never the box\'s or a device\'s: today, `created`, a deferral (from its day\'s start) and a deadline (reached at its day\'s END) all read it, and he changes it when he moves. A page writes by the box\'s contract or not at all: `gate::CLIENT_MIN` refuses an older bundle with 426. The page then says "Suzatary \\<version> is ready" with Reload, and the refused act was not sent (2026-10-06, the `instant-timezone-semantics` report). A wrong clock produces a future-dated object, which `verify` reports and the UI can say; rewriting it is the one thing the Prodrome refuses to do. Ordering is causal either way, but a fold reads events with `at <= t`, so a future-dated act is stored and not yet believed. Equal-instant contradictions resolve deterministically but arbitrarily, by canonical print.', rationale='Decided 2026-10-06 (the instant-timezone-semantics report).', supersedes=()))